</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><div class="codeblock3"><pre>Given Dictionary: {'apple': 20, 'banana': 14, 'watermelon': 2, 'kiwi': 12, 'oranges': 24}
Popped Value: 12
Updated Dictionary: {'apple': 20, 'banana': 14, 'watermelon': 2, 'oranges': 24}
</pre></div><p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p><p>Here, we are given a dictionary. We used the pop() method to remove the specified key. We have also mentioned the default value to be returned in case the specified key is not present in the dictionary. Since the key exists in the dictionary, the returned value is the value associated with the specified key.</p><h3 class="h3">Things to Remember</h3><p>Here are some key points to remember while using the Python dictionary pop() method:</p><ul class="points"><li>The pop() can be used in cases where you want to remove and retrieve a key's value.</li><li>Always check if the key exists - or just give it a default value and move on.</li><li>Always keep a backup of your original dictionary.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Conclusion</h2><p>In this <a href="python-tutorial">Python tutorial</a>, we learned that the dictionary pop() method is used to remove the specified key and return a value associated with it. If the specified key does not exist in the dictionary, it raises a KeyError error. Therefore, it is recommended to add a default value to the method. We used examples of using the dictionary pop() method in Python.</p><h2 class="h2">Python Dictionary pop() Method FAQs</h2><p><strong>1. What is the purpose of using the Dictionary pop() method in Python?</strong></p><p>The pop() is used to remove an element from the dictionary. It removes the element from the dictionary that is associated with the specified key.</p><p>Let's understand this using an example:</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="python" name="code">dict = {'Apple': 25, 'Banana': 220, 'Mango': 525, 'Blueberry': 217}    
# Calling method    
val = dict.pop('Apple')    
# Displaying result    
print( val )    
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><div class="codeblock3"><pre>25</pre></div><p><strong>2. What is the syntax of the pop() method?</strong></p><p>The syntax of the Dictionary pop() method in Python is as follows:</p><p><strong>Syntax:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="python" name="code">dictionary-name.pop(key, default-value)</textarea></div><p><strong>3. What does pop() return in Python?</strong></p><p>The pop() method in Python removes and returns the value associated with the specified key. If the given key is not present in the Dictionary, it throws a KeyError exception.</p><p><strong>4. Show a simple example of using the pop() method in Python.</strong></p><p><strong>Example:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="python" name="code">#creating a dictionary
